ues  but it gets halved for every power of  two you go out so the further you go out  the looser the approximation is  and we ran into this exact problem in  the world generation series if you  haven't seen that series i've been  building out a series on building out  entire earth-sized planets in javascript  and 3js  and with a mantissa of 23 bits you get  roughly 7 digits of precision in base  ten give or take i'm just kind of being  hand wavy here but what that means is  that when you've got one really big  number and one really small number like  in the video we talked about being at  one hundred thousand and trying to add  one millimeter or  0.001 then you just get a hundred  thousand because there simply isn't  enough precision to handle that we  talked to the start how if a is greater  than zero and b is greater than zero you  could end up with a plus b is equal to a  and that's why  another interesting property of floating  point numbers since we're talking about  how their approximations to the set of  real numbers  is that for smaller integers  they actually correspond exactly one to  one they exactly cover the set of  integer values up until a certain point  you can see here we can plug in a bunch  of integer values into the float here  let's say i want to represent a thousand  that's easy just turns out as 2 to the  power 9 times 1.95 blah blah blah it's  exactly representable in floating point  and it turns out since you've got 23  bits of precision in the mantissa you  can represent every single integer value  up to 2 to the power 24 which is kind of  neat that gives you up until the integer  value of about 16  777 thousand two sixteen but try adding  just one more to that and you can't  that's the limit and at one six seven  seven two one seven they can't be  represented anymore and floating point  will only cover even numbers and skip  every odd number so floats are fuzzy  representations of real numbers and  because they're not exact the math can  b